This directory documents the encounter that inspired my thoughts about recursive fiction. This wing should not be considered part of the estate… rather it is a sketchbook that helped model the Narratives wing. Essentially a popular AI writer put forth a challenge to have AI write fiction (and fail at it). I wanted to see if I could make it… not fail.

Erik Larson via LinkedIn:

"Some red meat from someone on the front lines writing and evaluating LLMs:

An LLM is interminably inserting abstractions that don’t anchor the meaning or the text.

Look, here’s your AI:

Jake shook his head, still grinning. "They make it fun, though.” Jake looked around, feeling the rush, the warmth of absurd camaraderie. Peanut shells, old friends, and debates that went nowhere but kept you grounded. It was good to lose himself in it, even for the night.

Here’s a human, who knows how to write:

Jake shook his head, still grinning. “They make it fun, though.” Jake looked around, the eclectic, crazy vibe of the Nut House. Peanut shells, old friends, and debates that went nowhere but gave you a brief respite from the interminable troubles waiting just outside the door. It was good to lose himself in it, even for the night.

See the difference?

I was going to teach a class on using an LLM to improve your writing. That was the original goal of my other Sub, By Erik J. Larson. But I got busy and couldn’t think of enough to say. I just want to write, not talk about it.

Now I should be teaching the class! Now I see the trouble. I laugh so hard my ribs hurt when I read contemporary discussion of LLMs “taking over.” You mean everyone gets worse?!!!!

Finally, the compression of the language space is different, and inferior, form a human to a machine, or LLM more specifically. This is a no b.s. way to see this without getting a Ph.D. in math or computer science. The phrases all seem right, but dfo’t really say anything. That’s s a bell curve compression.
I’m not. And neither should you be.

Cheers."


I elaborated!
